# Answer 12 · The Capitalistic Perspectives of Gender (CSS 2022)
@py question 2022-7

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Define capitalism and set out both perspectives — the market view and the critical view — with thinkers and evidence, then apply to Pakistan and conclude.
@ask WHAT ARE the CAPITALISTIC PERSPECTIVES of GENDER?
@ask EXPLAIN
(1) Introduction: capitalism shapes and is shaped by gender; two broad perspectives
(2) Capitalism explained: private property, markets, wage labour and profit
(3) The liberal market perspective: markets free women through jobs and income
(4) Smart economics: the World Bank's 2012 case for gender equality as growth
(5) The critical perspective, origins: Engels on private property and the family
(6) Separate spheres: industrial capitalism split home from work and made men breadwinners
(7) Unpaid labour and social reproduction: housework produces the worker for free
(8) Cheap and flexible labour: women as a reserve army; feminisation of labour
(9) Housewifisation and accumulation: Mies; Federici on the witch-hunts
(10) Global capitalism: export zones, supply chains, care chains
(11) Pakistan: home-based workers, textiles, cotton pickers, unpaid care
(12) Conclusion: capitalism can open doors for women but depends on their undervalued work
:::

## Decoding the question
**"What are the capitalistic perspectives of gender? Explain."** asks you to **identify** the different ways of understanding the relation between capitalism and gender, and to **explain** each with thinkers and evidence. There are at least two perspectives — the **liberal/market** view and the **critical/Marxist-feminist** view — and a strong answer presents both, then applies them.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Capitalism** is an economic system based on **private ownership**, **markets**, **wage labour** and **profit**. **Capitalistic perspectives of gender** are the ways scholars explain how capitalism **shapes** gender relations — and how it **depends on** them.

#### ii) The argument
There are two main perspectives. The **liberal (market)** perspective sees capitalism as a force that can **free** women through jobs and income. The **critical (Marxist and socialist-feminist)** perspective sees capitalism as **depending on and deepening** women's subordination, by profiting from their unpaid work at home and cheap work outside. The evidence supports parts of both.

### 2. Capitalism explained
Under capitalism, a few people own the **means of production** — land, factories, machines — and most people earn a living by selling their **labour** for wages. Firms compete to make **profit**. The system spread from Europe with industrialisation and colonialism, and today operates as a **global** economy. Its logic of profit and competition reaches into households, farms and informal work everywhere — including in Pakistan.

### 3. The liberal market perspective

#### i) The claim
Markets reward **skills and effort**, not sex. When women gain education and enter paid work, they earn **independent income**, gain bargaining power at home and escape family control.

#### ii) The barriers
Women's disadvantage comes from **barriers** — discriminatory laws, prejudice, lack of education and credit. Remove them and the market will treat women fairly.

#### iii) The evidence
Export jobs in Bangladesh and East Asia gave millions of young women **their first independent income**; research by **Naila Kabeer** (==2000==) found garment work delayed marriage and increased women's voice.

### 4. Smart economics
The World Bank's *World Development Report 2012: Gender Equality and Development* argued that gender equality is "**smart economics**": educating and employing women raises productivity and growth. Critics such as **Sylvia Chant and Caroline Sweetman** (==2012==) replied that this values women mainly as **instruments of growth**, not as rights-holders, and ignores unpaid care and power.

### 5. The critical perspective — origins
**Friedrich Engels** (*The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State*, ==1884==) argued that women's subordination began with **private property**: men who owned herds and land wanted to pass them to their own sons, and so controlled women's sexuality through monogamy — "**the world historical defeat of the female sex**". Capitalism, as a system of private property, inherited and used this subordination.

### 6. Separate spheres
Before industrial capitalism, most production took place in the **household**, where men and women both worked. Industrial capitalism moved production into **factories**, creating a **public** world of paid work, seen as men's, and a **private** world of unpaid domestic work, seen as women's. The ideology of **separate spheres** and the male **breadwinner** followed. Trade unions sometimes fought for a "**family wage**" for men that pushed women out of paid work (**Heidi Hartmann**, ==1979==).

### 7. Unpaid labour and social reproduction
Marxist feminists showed that housework is real **production**. **Margaret Benston** (==1969==) and **Mariarosa Dalla Costa and Selma James** (==1972==) argued that housework produces the most important commodity of all — **the worker** — every day, for free. This **social reproduction** lets employers pay wages that do not cover the full cost of keeping workers alive. Because it is unpaid, it is left out of GDP (**Marilyn Waring**, *If Women Counted*, ==1988==), and women who do it are seen as economically "inactive". **Silvia Federici** (==1975==) wrote: "They say it is love. We say it is unwaged work."

### 8. Cheap and flexible labour

#### i) Reserve army
Women are treated as a **reserve army of labour** — hired when needed, as in wartime factories, and dropped when not — and as "secondary earners", which justifies lower wages.

#### ii) Feminisation of labour
**Guy Standing** (==1989==) showed that global competition turned many jobs into **flexible, informal, low-paid** work increasingly filled by women.

### 9. Housewifisation and accumulation

::: thinker Maria Mies | *Patriarchy and Accumulation on a World Scale* (1986)
Studying the lace makers of **Narsapur**, India, Mies found that women making lace at home for world markets were counted as "housewives" doing leisure work, so they were paid very little. She called this "**housewifisation**": defining women as housewives so that their productive work can be cheapened. Capitalism, colonialism and patriarchy form **one system of accumulation**.
:::

The same logic applies wherever global firms subcontract to women working at home. **Silvia Federici** (*Caliban and the Witch*, ==2004==) argued that the rise of capitalism in Europe involved the **witch-hunts**, which destroyed women's control over reproduction and pushed them into unpaid domestic roles.

::: fig compare | Two capitalistic perspectives of gender.
## Liberal / market perspective
- Markets reward skill, not sex
- Paid work frees women
- Barriers: laws, prejudice, lack of skills
- Remedy: education, credit, equal opportunity
- Smart economics (World Bank 2012)
## Critical perspective
- Capitalism depends on women's unpaid work
- Women are cheap, flexible labour
- Barriers are built into the system
- Remedy: value care, labour rights, change the system
- Engels, Benston, Mies, Federici, Elson
:::

### 10. Global capitalism and gender
Global capitalism draws on women's labour in **export processing zones**, global **supply chains** (garments, electronics) and **global care chains** (migrant domestic workers). It creates jobs — and for many women, a first wage — but often insecure, unsafe and low-paid ones — as the **Rana Plaza** collapse (Bangladesh, ==2013==) and the **Ali Enterprises** fire (Karachi, ==2012==) showed.

### 11. The Pakistan angle

#### i) Home-based and textile work
Millions of Pakistani women stitch, embroider and pack at home for **piece rates** — housewifisation in action. **Sindh**'s **Home-Based Workers Act 2018** was the first to recognise them. Textile exports depend on low-paid labour.

#### ii) Agriculture and care
Women pick **cotton** for low seasonal wages and do most **unpaid care** — none of it counted in GDP.

#### iii) Limited entry
Female labour-force participation is only ==24.4%== (LFS 2024–25): capitalism in Pakistan has drawn fewer women into paid work than in Bangladesh — because of mobility limits, unsafe transport, harassment and lack of childcare. Where women do work, they are concentrated in the least protected parts of the economy.

### 12. Conclusion
The **liberal** perspective is right that paid work and markets can give women **income and independence**. The **critical** perspective is right that capitalism **depends on** women's unpaid and underpaid labour and does not value care. The two views meet in policy: women need **access to paid work** and **recognition, reduction and redistribution** of unpaid care, with labour rights that make capitalism work for them rather than on them.

## Why this answer scores
- Identifies **two perspectives** and explains each with thinkers and dates.
- The critical view is built step by step — Engels, separate spheres, social reproduction, reserve army, housewifisation.
- A **compare figure** sets the perspectives side by side.
- **Pakistani evidence** and a policy-oriented conclusion reconcile the two views.
